{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff ddb770352f35f2f55c740e9a8479686de3a251e5..HEAD -- POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff ddb770352f35f2f55c740e9a8479686de3a251e5..HEAD -- POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 061d04fb..4778725a 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -420,7 +420,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `usage` — aggregates automatic, project-scoped CLI and MCP usage events: calls, execution/semantic outcomes, latency, findings observed and stable finding lifecycle (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agents never maintain counters. Events are best-effort, local-only and stored outside the tracked worktree; raw arguments, output, paths, project/user identity and finding IDs are never persisted. `--since-days 0` includes all history; filter with `--tool` or `--surface`; MCP equivalent: `pose_usage`. This is product-usage evidence, not a DORA delivery outcome or an individual productivity score.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingest identity-free schema-v2 delivery events and calculate metrics for explicit production environments (`--environment`, default `production`). Deployments require `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidents require an environment; recovery includes only resolved `--caused-by-deployment` incidents. `adoption-metrics` aggregates specifications, roadmaps, and validation lifecycle rates.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — read-only, offline catalog inspection (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Matches directory entries against the maintained profile catalog (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — and **.NET**), reporting per profile: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (multiple managers present resolve by declared priority), `confidence` (`medium` under conflict), and whether the prerequisite native tool is on `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, never by executing a project file. Detected markers feed `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` the same way Node/Go/Rust/Java already do; the actual checks run through [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json) `python`/`dotnet` stacks. Python manager priority is expressed with `when.fileNotExistsAny` (skip when any higher-priority lockfile/marker exists) alongside the existing `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` predicates.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle. `discover`, `integrate` and `tech-debt` take `--no-persist` to print their result without writing `.pose/assessments/` or `.pose/state/`; in the sealed checkout of a delegated review (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) they never persist, `init`, `snapshot` and `request` refuse, and the review plan runs them with `--no-persist`, so a reviewer running a planned assessment does not fail its own run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — environment, dependency, native runtime, and POSE instance health diagnostics, plus governance that would otherwise fail only downstream: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (a selected profile demands a class no check may emit), `validate.class-producers` (a criterion accepts only classes no registered check produces), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks with no `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set`, and `<policy>.policy-keys` for nine policies (keys the engine does not read, i.e. a misspelled setting that silently does nothing).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — idempotent maintenance (list/archive/purge). Mutations require `--apply`. Reports housekeeping **never touches `history/`**: the JSONL is the source of truth for `recurrence-check` and `report`'s temporal comparisons. Defaults: stale = 120d, archive purge = 365d.\n - `amend` — append-only spec amendment history (`amendments.jsonl` beside a folder spec's `spec.md`, or `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` beside a flat dated spec; the spec is resolved through the store, so both layouts amend alike). `--baseline` snapshots every R-ID hash; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` acknowledges a material change; `--list` renders history and pending acknowledgments. On `done` specs with a history, `lint-spec` rejects any requirement whose current text is not acknowledged by an event — specs cannot be silently rewritten after evidence. `--nodes [--json]` prints the versioned contract-node projection: requirements, assumptions and decisions with namespace, content hash, state and relations. With `contract_nodes_version: 1` adopted in the review policy, `--baseline` writes a schema-2 R/A/D snapshot, `--ids` accepts A/D, `--change transition` acknowledges an allowed state change, `editorial` is refused when a node changed state or relations, and the gate also rejects an active decision resting on an invalidated or withdrawn assumption. Without the capability the gate stays requirement-only and a schema-2 event is refused; schema-1 logs keep their meaning.\n@@ -1071,9 +1071,11 @@ Preview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then\n \n Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n \n-**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/8` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/8` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n+**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n \n-**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+\n+**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there any process of the same user whose environment could not be read; on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause; `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt.\n \n **Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\n \ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 1f7e93c1..8aa07421 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -424,7 +424,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `usage` — agrega automaticamente eventos locais e por projeto de uso da CLI e do MCP: chamadas, outcomes de execução/semântica, latência, findings observados e ciclo de vida estável (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agentes nunca mantêm contadores. Os eventos são best-effort, somente locais e ficam fora da árvore versionada; argumentos, saída, paths, identidade de projeto/usuário e IDs crus de findings nunca são persistidos. `--since-days 0` inclui todo o histórico; filtre com `--tool` ou `--surface`; equivalente MCP: `pose_usage`. É evidência de uso do produto, não outcome DORA nem score individual de produtividade.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingerem eventos de entrega schema v2 sem identidade individual e calculam métricas para um ambiente de produção explícito (`--environment`, padrão `production`). Deployments exigem `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidentes exigem ambiente; recovery inclui apenas incidentes resolvidos com `--caused-by-deployment`. `adoption-metrics` agrega taxas de especificações, roadmaps e validação de ciclo de vida.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — inspeção de catálogo read-only e offline (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Casa entradas de diretório contra o catálogo mantido de perfis (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — e **.NET**), reportando por perfil: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (múltiplos managers presentes resolvem por prioridade declarada), `confidence` (`medium` sob conflito) e se a ferramenta nativa pré-requisito está no `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, nunca executando um arquivo do projeto. Markers detectados alimentam `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` do mesmo jeito que Node/Go/Rust/Java já fazem; os checks propriamente ditos rodam pelas stacks `python`/`dotnet` da [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json). A prioridade de manager Python é expressa com `when.fileNotExistsAny` (pular quando existir qualquer lockfile/marker de prioridade maior) ao lado dos predicados `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` já existentes.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities. `discover`, `integrate` e `tech-debt` aceitam `--no-persist` para imprimir o resultado sem gravar `.pose/assessments/` nem `.pose/state/`; no checkout selado de uma review delegada (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) eles nunca gravam, `init`, `snapshot` e `request` recusam, e o plano de review os roda com `--no-persist`, para que um revisor que roda um assessment planejado não derrube o próprio run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — diagnósticos de ambiente, dependências, runtime nativo e saúde da instância POSE, além de governança que só falharia mais adiante: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (um profile selecionado exige uma classe que nenhum check pode emitir), `validate.class-producers` (um critério aceita apenas classes que nenhum check registrado produz), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks sem `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set` e `<policy>.policy-keys` para nove políticas (chaves que o motor não lê, ou seja, uma configuração com erro de digitação que silenciosamente não faz nada).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — manutenção idempotente (listar/arquivar/expurgar). Mutações exigem `--apply`. O housekeeping de reports **nunca toca em `history/`**: o JSONL é a fonte de verdade para `recurrence-check` e comparações temporais de `report`. Defaults: stale = 120d, purga de arquivo = 365d.\n - `amend` — histórico append-only de emendas da spec (`amendments.jsonl` ao lado do `spec.md` de uma spec em pasta, ou `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` ao lado de uma spec flat datada; a spec é resolvida pelo store, então os dois layouts recebem emendas do mesmo modo). `--baseline` fotografa o hash de cada R-ID; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` reconhece uma mudança material; `--list` renderiza o histórico e os reconhecimentos pendentes. Em specs `done` com histórico, o `lint-spec` rejeita todo requisito cujo texto atual não esteja reconhecido por um evento — specs não podem ser reescritas em silêncio depois da evidência. `--nodes [--json]` mostra a projeção versionada de contract nodes: requisitos, premissas e decisões com namespace, hash de conteúdo, estado e relações. Com `contract_nodes_version: 1` adotado na policy de review, `--baseline` grava um snapshot R/A/D schema 2, `--ids` aceita A/D, `--change transition` reconhece uma mudança de estado permitida, `editorial` é recusado quando o nó mudou estado ou relações, e o gate também rejeita decisão ativa apoiada em premissa invalidada ou retirada. Sem a capability o gate segue só com requisitos e um evento schema 2 é recusado; logs schema 1 mantêm o significado.\n@@ -1068,9 +1068,11 @@ Visualize `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <nome> [--ttl <duração>]` e\n \n Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicitação completa de `pose review attest`. O issuer deriva do run os vínculos de principal/execução e assina a referência exata. Critérios, tools, evidências e preflight continuam obrigatórios. Draft negativo ou ambíguo não apoia aprovação; a atestação não pode anteceder o término do run ou sua prova assinada. Provas de adjudication/smoke vinculam seus kinds e não servem como atestações de review. Validar/aplicar automaticamente conclusões completas e tipadas pertence à capability, não à assinatura do run.\n \n-**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/8` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/8` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n+**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n \n-**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n+**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n+\n+**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux, depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria; cada um é fixado por um pidfd e conferido de novo antes do sinal. Ele espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza, declarando ali qualquer processo do mesmo usuário cujo ambiente não pôde ser lido; nas outras plataformas só o grupo de processos é encerrado; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa; `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo.\n \n **Requests de review delegada.** `pose review request <escopo|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <nome,...>] [--note-file <caminho>] [--apply]` abre uma action request de kind `delegated-review` que vincula o projeto, o worktree, o escopo, o bundle selado, o brief, as notas, as policies, os agentes aceitos e o destino da conclusão (uma atestação, um registro de adjudicação ou evidência de smoke). Nenhum ator a responde: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <chave> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <arquivo>] [--apply]`, ou a tool MCP `pose_action_fulfil`, submete um run concluído, e o motor registra o fulfillment só quando o run é do brief e do bundle do request, de um agente aceito, concluído com sua contenção, sua prova assinada verifica, seu ledger de tentativas está íntegro e o motor registrou sua conclusão no destino do request; o fulfillment é verificado de novo a cada leitura. `pose delegate review <escopo> --request <act-id>` roda o brief do request localmente e submete o run depois de concluído. Um run que rejeita cumpre o request sem aprovar nada; um bundle superado ou uma policy alterada o invalida, mesmo já cumprido, e ele é pedido de novo com `--supersedes <act-id>`, que o aposenta sob as regras de autoridade de supersession (`--authority <json>`, `--role <papel>`, `--requested-at` do preview) ou, quando o plano passa a reservar a review a uma pessoa, faz da escalação para a pessoa o seu sucessor; um retry com a mesma chave e o mesmo run não faz nada. Uma pessoa é chamada por exceção, como decision request ao papel maintainer: um plano que reserva a review a uma pessoa, um orçamento de tentativas esgotado, revisores em conflito e uma `business_decision` que um revisor levantou no draft. A prova de um run verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto: o worktree onde rodou fica registrado, não é exigido de quem lê, então um job de CI ou o Conductor cumpre o request com o mesmo envelope.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..69fcb01a\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@\n+//go:build linux\n+\n+package cli\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"os/exec\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"strconv\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"syscall\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n+)\n+\n+// Spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R1, R2, R4: a\n+// reviewer that ends while a command it started in a session of its own is\n+// still running leaves a run that completes, with that command ended before\n+// the cleanup and named in the run record.\n+func TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning(t *testing.T) {\n+\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"setsid\"); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Skip(\"setsid is not available\")\n+\t}\n+\troot := dispatchInstance(t)\n+\tpidFile := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"leftover.pid\")\n+\tscript := `cat >/dev/null; setsid sh -c 'echo $$ > \"` + pidFile + `\"; while :; do touch \"$TMPDIR/leftover\" 2>/dev/null; sleep 0.05; done' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & ` +\n+\t\t`i=0; while [ ! -s \"` + pidFile + `\" ] && [ $i -lt 100 ]; do sleep 0.05; i=$((i+1)); done; printf 'decision: approved\\n'`\n+\twriteReviewers(t, root, map[string]pose.ReviewerAdapter{\"fake\": {Command: []string{\"sh\", \"-c\", script}, Vendor: \"fixture\", Model: \"fixture\", Containment: &pose.ReviewerContainment{SchemaVersion: 1, Profile: \"trusted-process\"}}})\n+\tcode, out := runPose(t, root, \"delegate\", \"review\", \"spec:alpha\", \"--agent\", \"fake\")\n+\traw, err := os.ReadFile(pidFile)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the leftover never started: %v (%d %s)\", err, code, out)\n+\t}\n+\tpid, _ := strconv.Atoi(strings.TrimSpace(string(raw)))\n+\tt.Cleanup(func() { _ = syscall.Kill(pid, syscall.SIGKILL) })\n+\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(2 * time.Second)\n+\tfor syscall.Kill(pid, 0) == nil && time.Now().Before(deadline) {\n+\t\tif stat, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\"); err == nil && strings.Contains(string(stat), \") Z \") {\n+\t\t\tbreak\n+\t\t}\n+\t\ttime.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)\n+\t}\n+\tif stat, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\"); err == nil && !strings.Contains(string(stat), \") Z \") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the process the reviewer left running survived the run: %s\", stat)\n+\t}\n+\truns, _, _ := (pose.Store{Root: root}).ReadReviewRuns(\"\")\n+\tif len(runs) != 1 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"runs: %d (%d %s)\", len(runs), code, out)\n+\t}\n+\trun := runs[0]\n+\tif run.Status != pose.ReviewRunCompleted || run.Safety == nil || run.Safety.Operation.Outcome != pose.OutcomeCompleted {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the run did not complete with its cleanup: status=%s failure=%s safety=%+v\", run.Status, run.Failure, run.Safety)\n+\t}\n+\tif !strings.Contains(strings.Join(run.Safety.Limitations, \"\\n\"), \"process(es) the run left running\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the run record does not name the ended process: %v\", run.Safety.Limitations)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..bb4cfd7d\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,160 @@\n+//go:build linux\n+\n+package pose\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"encoding/json\"\n+\t\"errors\"\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"os/exec\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+)\n+\n+// Spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R5, R6: a dispatch\n+// that ended between its reservation and its run record leaves an attempt\n+// that blocks the bundle; once its owner is proven gone, cancel releases it\n+// with a failed run and its receipt, and the ledger reads again.\n+func TestAnAbandonedAttemptIsReleasedWithARecord(t *testing.T) {\n+\troot := t.TempDir()\n+\ts := Store{Root: root}\n+\tbundle := \"rvb-00000000000000a1\"\n+\ta := DelegatedReviewAttempt{SchemaVersion: 1, BundleID: bundle, BundleDigest: \"sha256:\" + strings.Repeat(\"a\", 64), Scope: \"spec:alpha\", Adapter: \"fake\", Ordinal: 1,\n+\t\tReservedAt: time.Now().Add(-time.Hour).UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano), Nonce: strings.Repeat(\"0\", 32)}\n+\ta.AttemptID = attemptIdentity(a)\n+\trel, _ := filepath.Rel(root, reviewAttemptPath(root, a))\n+\tif err := publishLedgerJSON(root, filepath.ToSlash(rel), a); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\t// The ledger refuses the bundle while the reservation has no receipt.\n+\tif _, err := s.readAttemptReceipt(a); err == nil || !strings.Contains(err.Error(), \"incomplete or unreadable\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unfinished reservation read as finished: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+\n+\t// The marker the interrupted dispatch left: its owner is a process that\n+\t// has exited.\n+\tdead := exec.Command(\"true\")\n+\tif err := dead.Run(); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\towner := StartLockOwner{SchemaVersion: StartLockOwnerSchemaVersion, Resource: \"delegate:\" + a.AttemptID, Worktree: root, Operation: \"pose delegate review\", PID: os.Getpid()}\n+\towner.Host, _ = os.Hostname()\n+\tidentifyStartLockProcess(&owner)\n+\towner.PID = dead.Process.Pid\n+\tmarker := DelegationActiveRun{SchemaVersion: 1, AttemptID: a.AttemptID, RunToken: strings.Repeat(\"b\", 32), Purpose: DelegationPurposeReview, Agent: \"fake\", Scope: a.Scope, BundleID: bundle,\n+\t\tStartedAt: a.ReservedAt, Owner: owner, HeartbeatAt: a.ReservedAt, HeartbeatEvery: 10}\n+\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\traw, _ := json.Marshal(marker)\n+\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, a.AttemptID+\".json\"), raw, 0o600); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\n+\trun, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(a.AttemptID, time.Now())\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tif run.Status != ReviewRunFailed || !strings.Contains(run.Failure, \"abandoned\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the release recorded %s: %s\", run.Status, run.Failure)\n+\t}\n+\tif _, err := s.readAttemptReceipt(a); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the released attempt still blocks the bundle: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+\tif left, _ := filepath.Glob(filepath.Join(dir, a.AttemptID+\"*\")); len(left) != 0 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the marker or its stall claim stayed: %v\", left)\n+\t}\n+\t// Released once: a second release finds nothing abandoned.\n+\tif _, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(a.AttemptID, time.Now()); !errors.Is(err, ErrAttemptNotAbandoned) {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a released attempt was released again: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// An attempt whose owner is alive is never released.\n+func TestARunningAttemptIsNotReleased(t *testing.T) {\n+\troot := t.TempDir()\n+\ts := Store{Root: root}\n+\tbundle := \"rvb-00000000000000a2\"\n+\t// Silent for an hour, so only the owner's liveness keeps it running.\n+\told := time.Now().Add(-time.Hour).UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)\n+\ta := DelegatedReviewAttempt{SchemaVersion: 1, BundleID: bundle, BundleDigest: \"sha256:\" + strings.Repeat(\"a\", 64), Scope: \"spec:alpha\", Adapter: \"fake\", Ordinal: 1, ReservedAt: old, Nonce: strings.Repeat(\"0\", 32)}\n+\ta.AttemptID = attemptIdentity(a)\n+\trel, _ := filepath.Rel(root, reviewAttemptPath(root, a))\n+\tif err := publishLedgerJSON(root, filepath.ToSlash(rel), a); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\towner := StartLockOwner{SchemaVersion: StartLockOwnerSchemaVersion, Resource: \"delegate:\" + a.AttemptID, Worktree: root, Operation: \"pose delegate review\", PID: os.Getpid()}\n+\towner.Host, _ = os.Hostname()\n+\tidentifyStartLockProcess(&owner)\n+\tmarker := DelegationActiveRun{SchemaVersion: 1, AttemptID: a.AttemptID, RunToken: strings.Repeat(\"d\", 32), Purpose: DelegationPurposeReview, Agent: \"fake\", Scope: \"spec:alpha\", BundleID: bundle, StartedAt: old, Owner: owner, HeartbeatAt: old, HeartbeatEvery: 10}\n+\tdir, _ := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n+\traw, _ := json.Marshal(marker)\n+\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\".json\"), raw, 0o600); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tif _, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(marker.AttemptID, time.Now()); !errors.Is(err, ErrAttemptNotAbandoned) {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a running attempt was released: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// Review round 1, N1: the dispatch marks the attempt active before it\n+// publishes the reservation, so no instant leaves a reservation without a\n+// marker that turns stalled.\n+func TestTheAttemptIsMarkedActiveBeforeItIsReserved(t *testing.T) {\n+\tvar checked bool\n+\tbeforeAttemptPublished = func(store Store, a DelegatedReviewAttempt) {\n+\t\tchecked = true\n+\t\tmarkers, _, err := store.ListDelegationActive()\n+\t\tfound := false\n+\t\tfor _, m := range markers {\n+\t\t\tfound = found || m.AttemptID == a.AttemptID\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif err != nil || !found {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"the reservation of %s is published with no active marker: %v\", a.AttemptID, err)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(reviewAttemptPath(store.Root, a)); !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"the reservation was published before the marker: %v\", err)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tt.Cleanup(func() { beforeAttemptPublished = func(Store, DelegatedReviewAttempt) {} })\n+\t_ = provenanceRunFixture(t, \"fixture-vendor\", \"fixture-model\")\n+\tif !checked {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"the dispatch never reached the reservation\")\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// A marker whose dispatch ended before publishing the reservation is removed\n+// by the release; ls names the cause and the release command (N2).\n+func TestAStalledMarkerWithoutReservationIsListedAndRemoved(t *testing.T) {\n+\troot := t.TempDir()\n+\ts := Store{Root: root}\n+\tdead := exec.Command(\"true\")\n+\tif err := dead.Run(); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\towner := StartLockOwner{SchemaVersion: StartLockOwnerSchemaVersion, Resource: \"delegate:x\", Worktree: root, Operation: \"pose delegate review\", PID: os.Getpid()}\n+\towner.Host, _ = os.Hostname()\n+\tidentifyStartLockProcess(&owner)\n+\towner.PID = dead.Process.Pid\n+\told := time.Now().Add(-time.Hour).UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)\n+\tmarker := DelegationActiveRun{SchemaVersion: 1, AttemptID: \"rat-\" + strings.Repeat(\"e\", 64), RunToken: strings.Repeat(\"f\", 32), Purpose: DelegationPurposeReview, Agent: \"fake\", Scope: \"spec:alpha\", BundleID: \"rvb-00000000000000a3\", StartedAt: old, Owner: owner, HeartbeatAt: old, HeartbeatEvery: 10}\n+\tdir, _ := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n+\traw, _ := json.Marshal(marker)\n+\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\".json\"), raw, 0o600); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tattempts, _, err := s.ListDelegationAttempts()\n+\tif err != nil || len(attempts) != 1 || attempts[0].State != DelegationStalled || !strings.Contains(attempts[0].Failure, DelegationAbandonedCause) || !strings.Contains(attempts[0].Failure, \"pose delegate cancel \"+marker.AttemptID) {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the abandoned attempt is not listed with its cause: %+v %v\", attempts, err)\n+\t}\n+\trun, err := s.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(marker.AttemptID, time.Now())\n+\tif err != nil || run.RunID != \"\" {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"release: %+v %v\", run, err)\n+\t}\n+\tif left, _ := filepath.Glob(filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\"*\")); len(left) != 0 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the marker stayed: %v\", left)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go\nindex dccd15d5..788bc3ec 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go\n@@ -131,6 +131,16 @@ func resolveReviewerContainment(a ReviewerAdapter) (*ReviewExecutionSafety, erro\n \tif runtime.GOOS == \"linux\" {\n \t\tif err := reviewProcessGroupAvailable(); err == nil {\n \t\t\tr.Capabilities[\"process-group-termination\"] = \"available\"\n+\t\t\t// A descendant that left the group is still ended while its\n+\t\t\t// environment keeps the run's sealed-checkout entry; one that\n+\t\t\t// clears it is not, so the guarantee is not \"available\" (spec\n+\t\t\t// pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R1, L1).\n+\t\t\tr.Capabilities[\"escaped-descendant-termination\"] = \"environment-marked\"\n+\t\t\tfor i, limit := range r.Limitations {\n+\t\t\t\tif limit == \"no memory/CPU quota or control of descendants escaping the process group\" {\n+\t\t\t\t\tr.Limitations[i] = \"no memory/CPU quota; descendants escaping the process group are ended only while they keep the run's sealed-checkout environment entry\"\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t}\n \t\t} else {\n \t\t\tr.Capabilities[\"process-group-termination\"] = \"unavailable\"\n \t\t\tr.Limitations = append(r.Limitations, \"process inventory not observable on this host\")\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go\nindex b2223b75..caefb8a1 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go\n@@ -294,7 +294,10 @@ func TestAdapterContainmentFailedCleanupContinuationRemainsFailed(t *testing.T)\n \t}\n }\n \n-func TestAdapterContainmentEscapedFiniteChildIsDisclosedNotContained(t *testing.T) {\n+// A child that escaped the process group but keeps the run's environment is\n+// ended with the run, and the guarantee is disclosed as environment-marked,\n+// never as available (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes).\n+func TestAdapterContainmentEscapedChildIsEndedWhileEnvironmentMarked(t *testing.T) {\n \tif runtime.GOOS != \"linux\" {\n \t\tt.Skip(\"Linux setsid limitation fixture\")\n \t}\n@@ -310,8 +313,8 @@ func TestAdapterContainmentEscapedFiniteChildIsDisclosedNotContained(t *testing.\n \t\ta.Containment = containmentPolicy()\n \t\ta.Command = []string{\"sh\", \"-c\", `cat >/dev/null; setsid sh -c 'printf ready > \"$3\"; n=0; while test \"$n\" -lt 200; do if test -e \"$1\"; then printf escaped > \"$2\"; exit 0; fi; n=$((n+1)); sleep 0.01; done' sh \"$1\" \"$2\" \"$3\" >/dev/null 2>&1 & n=0; while ! test -e \"$3\"; do n=$((n+1)); test \"$n\" -lt 100 || exit 2; sleep 0.01; done; printf 'decision: approved\\n'`, \"sh\", trigger, escaped, ready}\n \t})\n-\tif f.run.Status != ReviewRunCompleted || f.run.Safety.Capabilities[\"escaped-descendant-termination\"] != \"unavailable\" {\n-\t\tt.Fatal(\"escaped process was claimed contained\", f.run.Failure, f.run.Safety)\n+\tif f.run.Status != ReviewRunCompleted || f.run.Safety.Capabilities[\"escaped-descendant-termination\"] != \"environment-marked\" {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"escaped-process termination was not disclosed as environment-marked\", f.run.Failure, f.run.Safety)\n \t}\n \tif _, err := os.Stat(escaped); !os.IsNotExist(err) {\n \t\tt.Fatal(\"escape fixture ran before dispatch returned\", err)\n@@ -319,14 +322,10 @@ func TestAdapterContainmentEscapedFiniteChildIsDisclosedNotContained(t *testing.\n \tif err := os.WriteFile(trigger, []byte(\"release finite child\"), 0600); err != nil {\n \t\tt.Fatal(err)\n \t}\n-\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(time.Second)\n-\tfor time.Now().Before(deadline) {\n-\t\tif raw, err := os.ReadFile(escaped); err == nil && string(raw) == \"escaped\" {\n-\t\t\treturn\n-\t\t}\n-\t\ttime.Sleep(10 * time.Millisecond)\n+\ttime.Sleep(500 * time.Millisecond)\n+\tif _, err := os.Stat(escaped); !os.IsNotExist(err) {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"the escaped child outlived the run\", err)\n \t}\n-\tt.Fatal(\"escape fixture did not exercise the disclosed limitation\")\n }\n \n // Spec pose-review-cleanup-removes-go-build-leftovers: the Go build directory\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\nindex a4f69b9b..1a037471 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@ const (\n )\n \n var reviewBriefTemplates = map[string]string{\n-\tReviewBriefKindReview:       reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck,\n+\tReviewBriefKindReview:       reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands,\n \tReviewBriefKindAdjudication: adjudicationBriefTemplateTyped,\n \tReviewBriefKindSmoke:        smokeBriefTemplateTyped,\n }\n@@ -69,6 +69,16 @@ const (\n \t// that checks the draft with `pose review draft-check` before the run ends\n \t// (spec pose-review-readiness-before-seal R4).\n \treviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck = \"review-brief/8\"\n+\t// reviewBriefTemplateEngineTools was the current template: /8 plus the\n+\t// instruction to run the planned `pose` commands with the engine that\n+\t// dispatched the run, since the reviewer's shell may resolve an older\n+\t// installed `pose` that ignores flags such as --no-persist (spec\n+\t// pose-assess-leaves-sealed-checkouts-alone R5).\n+\treviewBriefTemplateEngineTools = \"review-brief/9\"\n+\t// reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands is the current template: /9 plus the\n+\t// boundary that every command the reviewer starts ends before it does\n+\t// (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R3).\n+\treviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands = \"review-brief/10\"\n \t// reviewBriefTemplateConclusions asked for the typed conclusion and stated its rules,\n \t// without the identifiers of the findings of earlier rounds.\n \t// reviewBriefTemplateConclusions (/4) asked for the typed conclusion and stated the rules\n@@ -84,7 +94,17 @@ const (\n // reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable reports /7 and the templates that keep its\n // text and add to it.\n func reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template string) bool {\n-\treturn template == reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable || template == reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck\n+\treturn template == reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable || reviewBriefFromDraftCheck(template)\n+}\n+\n+// reviewBriefFromDraftCheck reports /8 and the templates that keep its text.\n+func reviewBriefFromDraftCheck(template string) bool {\n+\treturn template == reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck || reviewBriefFromEngineTools(template)\n+}\n+\n+// reviewBriefFromEngineTools reports /9 and the templates that keep its text.\n+func reviewBriefFromEngineTools(template string) bool {\n+\treturn template == reviewBriefTemplateEngineTools || template == reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands\n }\n \n // reviewBriefAsksConclusion reports a template that asks for the typed conclusion.\n@@ -127,7 +147,7 @@ func reviewBriefStatesRules(template string) bool {\n // briefed with before the current one, newest first; a run briefed with one\n // is re-rendered with it to verify and to continue (the brief a run was sent\n // never changes under it).\n-var reviewBriefEarlierTemplates = []string{reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable, reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules, reviewBriefTemplateRoundIDs, reviewBriefTemplateConclusions, reviewBriefTemplateTypedConclusion, reviewBriefTemplateRounds, reviewBriefTemplateFirst}\n+var reviewBriefEarlierTemplates = []string{reviewBriefTemplateEngineTools, reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck, reviewBriefTemplateAllNotApplicable, reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules, reviewBriefTemplateRoundIDs, reviewBriefTemplateConclusions, reviewBriefTemplateTypedConclusion, reviewBriefTemplateRounds, reviewBriefTemplateFirst}\n \n // briefEarlierTemplates are, per kind, the templates a run may have been\n // briefed with before the current one, newest first.\n@@ -319,6 +339,9 @@ func renderReviewBriefBody(bundle ReviewBundle, kind, template, notes string, sm\n \tline(\"- Do not record an attestation: POSE records the conclusion of a verified run.\")\n \tline(\"- Do not reveal secrets or key material you come across.\")\n \tline(\"- If something you need is missing, say what and stop, rather than assuming it.\")\n+\tif template == reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands {\n+\t\tline(\"- Let every command you start finish before you end, and do not leave anything running in the background: POSE ends what is left and records it, and a long run left behind costs time you could have used.\")\n+\t}\n \tline(\"\")\n \n \tline(\"## Change\")\n@@ -375,6 +398,10 @@ func renderReviewBriefBody(bundle ReviewBundle, kind, template, notes string, sm\n \tif len(plan.Tools) > 0 {\n \t\tline(\"## Tools the plan requires\")\n \t\tline(\"\")\n+\t\tif reviewBriefFromEngineTools(template) {\n+\t\t\tline(\"Run each `pose` command below as `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\"` followed by the same arguments: it is the engine that sealed and dispatched this run. The `pose` your shell resolves may be an older installed engine that ignores flags such as `--no-persist` and writes into this sealed checkout, which fails the run.\")\n+\t\t\tline(\"\")\n+\t\t}\n \t\ttools := append([]ReviewPlanTool(nil), plan.Tools...)\n \t\tsort.SliceStable(tools, func(i, j int) bool { return tools[i].ID+tools[i].Component < tools[j].ID+tools[j].Component })\n \t\tfor _, t := range tools {\n@@ -485,7 +512,7 @@ func renderReviewBriefBody(bundle ReviewBundle, kind, template, notes string, sm\n \t\t\tif reviewBriefStatesRules(template) {\n \t\t\t\tline(\"%d. %s\", next+2, briefRulesText(template, bundle))\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\tif template == reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck {\n+\t\t\tif reviewBriefFromDraftCheck(template) {\n \t\t\t\tline(\"%d. Before you finish, check your final message: pipe it to `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\" review draft-check --draft -`, the engine that dispatched this run (it reads this run's bundle and round, writes nothing and works in a read-only sandbox) and fix every reason it reports. A draft it refuses is refused at conclusion, and the round is spent.\", next+3)\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..c611ce9d\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@\n+//go:build !linux\n+\n+package pose\n+\n+// reapSealedCheckoutProcesses has no portable way to read another process's\n+// environment; outside Linux the process-group kill is the only termination.\n+func reapSealedCheckoutProcesses(string) ([]string, int, error) { return nil, 0, nil }\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\nindex f4665a92..18dc5363 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n@@ -420,7 +420,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `usage` — aggregates automatic, project-scoped CLI and MCP usage events: calls, execution/semantic outcomes, latency, findings observed and stable finding lifecycle (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agents never maintain counters. Events are best-effort, local-only and stored outside the tracked worktree; raw arguments, output, paths, project/user identity and finding IDs are never persisted. `--since-days 0` includes all history; filter with `--tool` or `--surface`; MCP equivalent: `pose_usage`. This is product-usage evidence, not a DORA delivery outcome or an individual productivity score.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingest identity-free schema-v2 delivery events and calculate metrics for explicit production environments (`--environment`, default `production`). Deployments require `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidents require an environment; recovery includes only resolved `--caused-by-deployment` incidents. `adoption-metrics` aggregates specifications, roadmaps, and validation lifecycle rates.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — read-only, offline catalog inspection (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Matches directory entries against the maintained profile catalog (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — and **.NET**), reporting per profile: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (multiple managers present resolve by declared priority), `confidence` (`medium` under conflict), and whether the prerequisite native tool is on `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, never by executing a project file. Detected markers feed `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` the same way Node/Go/Rust/Java already do; the actual checks run through [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json) `python`/`dotnet` stacks. Python manager priority is expressed with `when.fileNotExistsAny` (skip when any higher-priority lockfile/marker exists) alongside the existing `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` predicates.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle. `discover`, `integrate` and `tech-debt` take `--no-persist` to print their result without writing `.pose/assessments/` or `.pose/state/`; in the sealed checkout of a delegated review (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) they never persist, `init`, `snapshot` and `request` refuse, and the review plan runs them with `--no-persist`, so a reviewer running a planned assessment does not fail its own run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — environment, dependency, native runtime, and POSE instance health diagnostics, plus governance that would otherwise fail only downstream: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (a selected profile demands a class no check may emit), `validate.class-producers` (a criterion accepts only classes no registered check produces), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks with no `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set`, and `<policy>.policy-keys` for nine policies (keys the engine does not read, i.e. a misspelled setting that silently does nothing).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — idempotent maintenance (list/archive/purge). Mutations require `--apply`. Reports housekeeping **never touches `history/`**: the JSONL is the source of truth for `recurrence-check` and `report`'s temporal comparisons. Defaults: stale = 120d, archive purge = 365d.\n - `amend` — append-only spec amendment history (`amendments.jsonl` beside a folder spec's `spec.md`, or `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` beside a flat dated spec; the spec is resolved through the store, so both layouts amend alike). `--baseline` snapshots every R-ID hash; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` acknowledges a material change; `--list` renders history and pending acknowledgments. On `done` specs with a history, `lint-spec` rejects any requirement whose current text is not acknowledged by an event — specs cannot be silently rewritten after evidence. `--nodes [--json]` prints the versioned contract-node projection: requirements, assumptions and decisions with namespace, content hash, state and relations. With `contract_nodes_version: 1` adopted in the review policy, `--baseline` writes a schema-2 R/A/D snapshot, `--ids` accepts A/D, `--change transition` acknowledges an allowed state change, `editorial` is refused when a node changed state or relations, and the gate also rejects an active decision resting on an invalidated or withdrawn assumption. Without the capability the gate stays requirement-only and a schema-2 event is refused; schema-1 logs keep their meaning.\n@@ -1058,9 +1058,11 @@ Preview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then\n \n Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n \n-**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/8` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/8` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n+**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n \n-**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+\n+**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there any process of the same user whose environment could not be read; on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause; `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt.\n \n **Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 1f7e93c1..8aa07421 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -424,7 +424,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `usage` — agrega automaticamente eventos locais e por projeto de uso da CLI e do MCP: chamadas, outcomes de execução/semântica, latência, findings observados e ciclo de vida estável (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agentes nunca mantêm contadores. Os eventos são best-effort, somente locais e ficam fora da árvore versionada; argumentos, saída, paths, identidade de projeto/usuário e IDs crus de findings nunca são persistidos. `--since-days 0` inclui todo o histórico; filtre com `--tool` ou `--surface`; equivalente MCP: `pose_usage`. É evidência de uso do produto, não outcome DORA nem score individual de produtividade.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingerem eventos de entrega schema v2 sem identidade individual e calculam métricas para um ambiente de produção explícito (`--environment`, padrão `production`). Deployments exigem `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidentes exigem ambiente; recovery inclui apenas incidentes resolvidos com `--caused-by-deployment`. `adoption-metrics` agrega taxas de especificações, roadmaps e validação de ciclo de vida.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — inspeção de catálogo read-only e offline (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Casa entradas de diretório contra o catálogo mantido de perfis (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — e **.NET**), reportando por perfil: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (múltiplos managers presentes resolvem por prioridade declarada), `confidence` (`medium` sob conflito) e se a ferramenta nativa pré-requisito está no `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, nunca executando um arquivo do projeto. Markers detectados alimentam `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` do mesmo jeito que Node/Go/Rust/Java já fazem; os checks propriamente ditos rodam pelas stacks `python`/`dotnet` da [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json). A prioridade de manager Python é expressa com `when.fileNotExistsAny` (pular quando existir qualquer lockfile/marker de prioridade maior) ao lado dos predicados `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` já existentes.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities. `discover`, `integrate` e `tech-debt` aceitam `--no-persist` para imprimir o resultado sem gravar `.pose/assessments/` nem `.pose/state/`; no checkout selado de uma review delegada (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) eles nunca gravam, `init`, `snapshot` e `request` recusam, e o plano de review os roda com `--no-persist`, para que um revisor que roda um assessment planejado não derrube o próprio run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — diagnósticos de ambiente, dependências, runtime nativo e saúde da instância POSE, além de governança que só falharia mais adiante: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (um profile selecionado exige uma classe que nenhum check pode emitir), `validate.class-producers` (um critério aceita apenas classes que nenhum check registrado produz), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks sem `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set` e `<policy>.policy-keys` para nove políticas (chaves que o motor não lê, ou seja, uma configuração com erro de digitação que silenciosamente não faz nada).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — manutenção idempotente (listar/arquivar/expurgar). Mutações exigem `--apply`. O housekeeping de reports **nunca toca em `history/`**: o JSONL é a fonte de verdade para `recurrence-check` e comparações temporais de `report`. Defaults: stale = 120d, purga de arquivo = 365d.\n - `amend` — histórico append-only de emendas da spec (`amendments.jsonl` ao lado do `spec.md` de uma spec em pasta, ou `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` ao lado de uma spec flat datada; a spec é resolvida pelo store, então os dois layouts recebem emendas do mesmo modo). `--baseline` fotografa o hash de cada R-ID; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` reconhece uma mudança material; `--list` renderiza o histórico e os reconhecimentos pendentes. Em specs `done` com histórico, o `lint-spec` rejeita todo requisito cujo texto atual não esteja reconhecido por um evento — specs não podem ser reescritas em silêncio depois da evidência. `--nodes [--json]` mostra a projeção versionada de contract nodes: requisitos, premissas e decisões com namespace, hash de conteúdo, estado e relações. Com `contract_nodes_version: 1` adotado na policy de review, `--baseline` grava um snapshot R/A/D schema 2, `--ids` aceita A/D, `--change transition` reconhece uma mudança de estado permitida, `editorial` é recusado quando o nó mudou estado ou relações, e o gate também rejeita decisão ativa apoiada em premissa invalidada ou retirada. Sem a capability o gate segue só com requisitos e um evento schema 2 é recusado; logs schema 1 mantêm o significado.\n@@ -1068,9 +1068,11 @@ Visualize `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <nome> [--ttl <duração>]` e\n \n Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicitação completa de `pose review attest`. O issuer deriva do run os vínculos de principal/execução e assina a referência exata. Critérios, tools, evidências e preflight continuam obrigatórios. Draft negativo ou ambíguo não apoia aprovação; a atestação não pode anteceder o término do run ou sua prova assinada. Provas de adjudication/smoke vinculam seus kinds e não servem como atestações de review. Validar/aplicar automaticamente conclusões completas e tipadas pertence à capability, não à assinatura do run.\n \n-**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/8` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/8` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n+**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n \n-**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n+**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n+\n+**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux, depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria; cada um é fixado por um pidfd e conferido de novo antes do sinal. Ele espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza, declarando ali qualquer processo do mesmo usuário cujo ambiente não pôde ser lido; nas outras plataformas só o grupo de processos é encerrado; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa; `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo.\n \n **Requests de review delegada.** `pose review request <escopo|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <nome,...>] [--note-file <caminho>] [--apply]` abre uma action request de kind `delegated-review` que vincula o projeto, o worktree, o escopo, o bundle selado, o brief, as notas, as policies, os agentes aceitos e o destino da conclusão (uma atestação, um registro de adjudicação ou evidência de smoke). Nenhum ator a responde: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <chave> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <arquivo>] [--apply]`, ou a tool MCP `pose_action_fulfil`, submete um run concluído, e o motor registra o fulfillment só quando o run é do brief e do bundle do request, de um agente aceito, concluído com sua contenção, sua prova assinada verifica, seu ledger de tentativas está íntegro e o motor registrou sua conclusão no destino do request; o fulfillment é verificado de novo a cada leitura. `pose delegate review <escopo> --request <act-id>` roda o brief do request localmente e submete o run depois de concluído. Um run que rejeita cumpre o request sem aprovar nada; um bundle superado ou uma policy alterada o invalida, mesmo já cumprido, e ele é pedido de novo com `--supersedes <act-id>`, que o aposenta sob as regras de autoridade de supersession (`--authority <json>`, `--role <papel>`, `--requested-at` do preview) ou, quando o plano passa a reservar a review a uma pessoa, faz da escalação para a pessoa o seu sucessor; um retry com a mesma chave e o mesmo run não faz nada. Uma pessoa é chamada por exceção, como decision request ao papel maintainer: um plano que reserva a review a uma pessoa, um orçamento de tentativas esgotado, revisores em conflito e uma `business_decision` que um revisor levantou no draft. A prova de um run verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto: o worktree onde rodou fica registrado, não é exigido de quem lê, então um job de CI ou o Conductor cumpre o request com o mesmo envelope.\n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
