{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_policy.go | sed -n '68,93p'; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go | sed -n '325,410p;500,520p'; rg -n 'Runs that leave work behind|process whose environment|review-brief/10|A review|R1-N5' POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md | tail -30\"","aggregated_output":"    68\t\t\treturn DelegationPurposeAdjudicate\n    69\t\tcase \"smoke\":\n    70\t\t\treturn DelegationPurposeSmoke\n    71\t\tdefault:\n    72\t\t\treturn DelegationPurposeReview\n    73\t\t}\n    74\t}\n    75\t\n    76\t// DelegationKindForPurpose maps a review-path purpose to its brief kind.\n    77\tfunc DelegationKindForPurpose(purpose string) string {\n    78\t\tswitch purpose {\n    79\t\tcase DelegationPurposeAdjudicate:\n    80\t\t\treturn \"adjudication\"\n    81\t\tcase DelegationPurposeSmoke:\n    82\t\t\treturn \"smoke\"\n    83\t\tdefault:\n    84\t\t\treturn \"review\"\n    85\t\t}\n    86\t}\n    87\t\n    88\t// ReviewerEffort maps an effort level to the argv fragment that stands where\n    89\t// the template has the whole element \"{effort}\".\n    90\ttype ReviewerEffort struct {\n    91\t\tDefault string              `json:\"default\"`\n    92\t\tLevels  map[string][]string `json:\"levels\"`\n    93\t}\n   325\t\tswitch kind {\n   326\t\tcase ReviewBriefKindReview:\n   327\t\t\tline(\"Review the change below against the scope's requirements and the criteria the plan owes. Read the code and run the tests you need; judge each criterion with your own evidence.\")\n   328\t\tcase ReviewBriefKindAdjudication:\n   329\t\t\tline(\"Give an independent verdict on the questions the scope records (its decisions, outcomes and requirement trace), using only the sealed evidence and what you can reproduce. Say for each whether the record holds, and why.\")\n   330\t\tcase ReviewBriefKindSmoke:\n   331\t\t\tline(\"Exercise each delivered surface of the scope from its entrypoint, as a user of it would, and report what you observe against what the spec says should happen. Do not review the code beyond what you need to run it.\")\n   332\t\t}\n   333\t\tline(\"\")\n   334\t\n   335\t\tline(\"## Boundaries\")\n   336\t\tline(\"\")\n   337\t\tline(\"- Report every defect with file, line and severity (high, medium, low). Do not fix it.\")\n   338\t\tline(\"- Do not change the repository: no edits, commits, pushes, tags or releases.\")\n   339\t\tline(\"- Do not record an attestation: POSE records the conclusion of a verified run.\")\n   340\t\tline(\"- Do not reveal secrets or key material you come across.\")\n   341\t\tline(\"- If something you need is missing, say what and stop, rather than assuming it.\")\n   342\t\tif template == reviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands {\n   343\t\t\tline(\"- Let every command you start finish before you end, and do not leave anything running in the background: POSE ends what is left and records it, and a long run left behind costs time you could have used.\")\n   344\t\t}\n   345\t\tline(\"\")\n   346\t\n   347\t\tline(\"## Change\")\n   348\t\tline(\"\")\n   349\t\tline(\"- range: %s..%s\", orNone(subject.Base), orNone(subject.Head))\n   350\t\tline(\"- change sets: %s\", orNone(strings.Join(subject.ChangeSets, \", \")))\n   351\t\tentries := append([]ReviewBundleSubjectEntry(nil), subject.Entries...)\n   352\t\tsort.Slice(entries, func(i, j int) bool { return entryPath(entries[i]) < entryPath(entries[j]) })\n   353\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n   354\t\t\tline(\"- %s %s (%s)\", e.Action, entryPath(e), e.Class)\n   355\t\t}\n   356\t\tline(\"\")\n   357\t\n   358\t\tif smoke != nil {\n   359\t\t\tline(\"## Surfaces to run\")\n   360\t\t\tline(\"\")\n   361\t\t\tif len(smoke.Surfaces) == 0 {\n   362\t\t\t\tline(\"The scope declares no delivery targets. Report that there is nothing to smoke and stop.\")\n   363\t\t\t}\n   364\t\t\tfor _, t := range smoke.Surfaces {\n   365\t\t\t\tline(\"- %s (module %s, profile %s): start from `%s`\", t.Ref, orNone(t.Module), orNone(t.Profile), orNone(t.Entrypoint))\n   366\t\t\t}\n   367\t\t\tline(\"\")\n   368\t\t\tline(\"## Expected observations\")\n   369\t\t\tline(\"\")\n   370\t\t\tline(\"Observe each requirement through the surfaces above, as their user would see it.\")\n   371\t\t\tline(\"\")\n   372\t\t\tfor _, r := range smoke.Requirements {\n   373\t\t\t\tline(\"- %s\", r)\n   374\t\t\t}\n   375\t\t\tif len(smoke.Requirements) == 0 {\n   376\t\t\t\tline(\"- none recorded in the spec; report that and stop.\")\n   377\t\t\t}\n   378\t\t\tline(\"\")\n   379\t\t}\n   380\t\n   381\t\tline(\"## Criteria to answer\")\n   382\t\tline(\"\")\n   383\t\tcriteria := append([]ReviewPlanCriterion(nil), plan.Criteria...)\n   384\t\tsort.Slice(criteria, func(i, j int) bool { return criteria[i].ID < criteria[j].ID })\n   385\t\tfor _, c := range criteria {\n   386\t\t\trequired := \"optional\"\n   387\t\t\tif c.Required {\n   388\t\t\t\trequired = \"required\"\n   389\t\t\t}\n   390\t\t\tevidence := \"\"\n   391\t\t\tif len(c.EvidenceClasses) > 0 {\n   392\t\t\t\tevidence = \"; evidence: \" + strings.Join(c.EvidenceClasses, \", \")\n   393\t\t\t}\n   394\t\t\tline(\"- %s (%s, %s%s): %s\", c.ID, ReviewCriterionKind(c), required, evidence, c.Description)\n   395\t\t}\n   396\t\tline(\"\")\n   397\t\n   398\t\tif len(plan.Tools) > 0 {\n   399\t\t\tline(\"## Tools the plan requires\")\n   400\t\t\tline(\"\")\n   401\t\t\tif reviewBriefFromEngineTools(template) {\n   402\t\t\t\tline(\"Run each `pose` command below as `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\"` followed by the same arguments: it is the engine that sealed and dispatched this run. The `pose` your shell resolves may be an older installed engine that ignores flags such as `--no-persist` and writes into this sealed checkout, which fails the run.\")\n   403\t\t\t\tline(\"\")\n   404\t\t\t}\n   405\t\t\ttools := append([]ReviewPlanTool(nil), plan.Tools...)\n   406\t\t\tsort.SliceStable(tools, func(i, j int) bool { return tools[i].ID+tools[i].Component < tools[j].ID+tools[j].Component })\n   407\t\t\tfor _, t := range tools {\n   408\t\t\t\tcomponent := \"\"\n   409\t\t\t\tif t.Component != \"\" {\n   410\t\t\t\t\tcomponent = \" [\" + t.Component + \"]\"\n   500\t\tif plan.Structure != nil && len(plan.Structure.Material) > 0 {\n   501\t\t\tline(\"3. For each structural fact: the requirement or decision it maps to.\")\n   502\t\t\tline(\"4. Every defect, with file, line and severity.\")\n   503\t\t\tnext = 5\n   504\t\t} else {\n   505\t\t\tline(\"3. Every defect, with file, line and severity.\")\n   506\t\t\tnext = 4\n   507\t\t}\n   508\t\tif round != nil {\n   509\t\t\tline(\"%d. At the end, the draft POSE reads: one fenced block opened with ```pose-draft holding a JSON object with `schema` %q, `decision` (the decision above), `findings` (each with `id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line` and `summary`)%s. Everything outside the block is yours and is not read.\", next, ReviewDraftSchema, roundFields(*round, template))\n   510\t\t\tif conclusion {\n   511\t\t\t\tline(\"%d. In the same block, the conclusion POSE records as the attestation: `criteria` (one entry per criterion above, each with `id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>` — a class the criterion asks for when it names one — and `rationale` for not-applicable), and `tools` (one entry per tool above, each with `id`, `component` when it has one, `disposition`, `evidence` and `rationale`). A follow-up finding that should be accepted as a risk carries `action`, `owner` and `review_by`; any other finding stays open. A criterion or tool you do not answer is not answered for you.\", next+1)\n   512\t\t\t\tif reviewBriefStatesRules(template) {\n   513\t\t\t\t\tline(\"%d. %s\", next+2, briefRulesText(template, bundle))\n   514\t\t\t\t}\n   515\t\t\t\tif reviewBriefFromDraftCheck(template) {\n   516\t\t\t\t\tline(\"%d. Before you finish, check your final message: pipe it to `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\" review draft-check --draft -`, the engine that dispatched this run (it reads this run's bundle and round, writes nothing and works in a read-only sandbox) and fix every reason it reports. A draft it refuses is refused at conclusion, and the round is spent.\", next+3)\n   517\t\t\t\t}\n   518\t\t\t}\n   519\t\t}\n   520\t\tif template == reviewBriefTemplateRiskRules || reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template) {\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1071:**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux, depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria; cada um é fixado por um pidfd e conferido de novo antes do sinal. Ele espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza, declarando ali qualquer processo do mesmo usuário cujo ambiente não pôde ser lido; nas outras plataformas só o grupo de processos é encerrado; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa; `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1071:**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux, depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria; cada um é fixado por um pidfd e conferido de novo antes do sinal. Ele espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza, declarando ali qualquer processo do mesmo usuário cujo ambiente não pôde ser lido; nas outras plataformas só o grupo de processos é encerrado; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa; `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:877:unrelated off-capability work stays off. A reviewer prefix alone cannot approve\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:1061:**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:1065:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there any process of the same user whose environment could not be read; on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause; `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt.\nPOSE.md:890:unrelated off-capability work stays off. A reviewer prefix alone cannot approve\nPOSE.md:1074:**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\nPOSE.md:1078:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there any process of the same user whose environment could not be read; on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause; `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
